What a diagram of two models is
Fig. 1.2 is the figure of a superposition: two models in one plane. It is drawn so that a parallelogram can be seen, and the parallelogram is the place of an argument. The diagram does not state the argument and does not state its hypotheses; the prose beside it does, in part. Three things are therefore distinguished:

the superposition: the two models with one earth, one apsidal direction, one mean anomaly;
the hypotheses under which something holds across them (R′ = R, r = e);
the derivation that reaches the equivalence from the hypotheses, step by step.

The printed figure shows the first, is drawn under the second, and exhibits the scene of the third.

What follows from the texts

a check per kind of text (Checking);
a specification sheet, a still figure (SVG, Typst) and a browser animation (Outputs, Runtime);
a reference address for every text and each of its parts, fr:astra/&lt;text&gt;/&lt;object&gt; (References);
a comparison of two models or two parametrisations (Comparison);
a deep reading of a source’s pages: every assertion quoted, formalised, recomputed (Deep reading).

What the builder refuses
The command fails, and lists the gaps, when

the first words of a span do not occur exactly once on the page, or the last words do not follow them;
a page has no page XML or no stored translation;
no paragraph of the reference register carries the passage (the register gives the paragraph addresses, fr:&lt;collection&gt;/&lt;document&gt;/&lt;page&gt;/pNN; the service must answer);
a step of the derivation stands beside no passage, or a passage names a step that does not exist;
a correction is not found exactly once, or cuts through a formula.

